## Chapter 1.6: Bootstrap

“Please write your details here, and your signature here, …_Citizen_ Huo.” Nadya, sitting on the other side of her desk partition, found it visibly difficult to address him with such an alienating title.

With Tarasov observing from just behind her shoulder, she had to maintain absolute professionalism. Right now, she had to be the prim and proper Militsiya officer on duty. Whatever warm camaraderie she had shared with Luka this morning had to be tucked away for the sake of procedural impartiality.

“Citizen Huo, if the autopsy from Vladivostok arrives and confirms the exact name you gave us, I will be required to formally issue an arrest warrant. Do you understand, and are you willing to comply?” Tarasov asked a question that permitted only one answer.

“…Yes, Comrade Major,” Luka answered, his voice weighed down with defeat.

Luka set his stylus pen down just as he set down his hope. This morning had been a rollercoaster that ended in a complete derailment. He was beginning to regret ever stepping out of his dormitory. The premonition was already a crushing burden; now he faced the likelihood of being detained and interrogated for the coming week. What if something happened to Alex in the meantime?

Nadya, on the other hand, was typing unusually slowly on her keyboard, as if deliberately trying to buy time.

Then the window of opportunity arrived. A patrol officer stepped up to Tarasov to consult him on an urgent precinct matter. Seizing the distraction, Nadya glanced across the counter. “Citizen Huo, please verify if the details I entered are correct.”

Luka looked down at the e-paper travel ban docket already resting on his side of the partition. Everything appeared standard—until a fresh line of text bloomed across the lower margin: _“Let’s talk after my shift. 1 PM, Cafe Stela.”_

A quiet spark of hope flickered in Luka’s chest. Nadya was still on his side, or at least open enough to hear him out. He had to admit, he desperately needed an ally right now.

Luka gave her a subtle, affirming nod. Nadya quickly cleared the secret message from the buffer, took back the e-paper, and proceeded to transcribe the statement onto a traditional physical form per post-Ripple compliance, acting as if nothing conspiratorial had just occurred under her superior’s watch.

Once finished, she passed the paper copy and a ballpoint pen to Luka for his ink signature. Just then, Tarasov finished his discussion with the officer. Noticing the signed document on the counter, the Major offered one final reminder:

“Very well, Citizen Huo. You may return to your routine for now. However, I expect your full cooperation should we decide to escalate this inquiry. Understood?”

“Yes, Comrade Major,” Luka replied, though this time his tone wasn't quite as broken.

Tarasov gave a curt nod before turning on his heel to return to his office.

Nadya offered Luka a discreet wink. He returned a small nod before pushing open the heavy storm doors of the precinct and stepping back out into the cold.

There were still nearly three hours before the scheduled meetup at the cafe. He could have headed back to the dormitory to rest, but any chance of that vanished the moment he cleared the steps.

“Little Huo!”

Lin Chen came running out from the library across the street, shouting and waving both arms to catch his attention.

She had followed him all the way to the precinct, as expected of a journalist. Normally, he might have found her persistent presence a little overwhelming, but in his current vulnerable state, he would have hugged anyone who spoke a single word of Chinese to him.

He crossed the snow-packed street to meet her, pleasantly surprised when she immediately pulled him into a warm, reassuring hug. “_Was it written on my face?_” he thought.

“Now, now, before you say anything, let’s get inside,” Lin said, pulling back. “I’ve had quite enough of this cold for one morning.”

They found a quiet place to sit down and talk about everything that had happened, starting with his vision about Alex all the way to the scene at the scrapyard. Lin was totally in awe the entire time, jotting brisk notes on her laptop.

“…And that’s about it,” Luka concluded with an exhausted sigh. “You’re only the second person I’ve shared this with.”

“It’s still quite hard to believe… but you’re not the type to joke around and get yourself into trouble,” she looked at him with genuine sincerity. “If you need help, Little Huo, you’ve got me in your corner, alright?”

“Thank you, Sis…” He truly valued her care.

“Compatriots need to help each other, especially in a foreign land like this. If they abuse their power here…” She smirked with sharp confidence. “They’re going to find out what a real PR disaster looks like!”

She gestured, parting her hands dramatically. “Headline: _Chinese Researcher Arrested in Pogranichny Based on Unverifiable ‘Visions.’_ See how their logic can be spun against them?”

Luka let out a laugh, casually joking back, “Aren’t you supposed to be impartial?”

“Heh, who told you? Being partially impartial is the essence of journalism!”

They shared a lighthearted giggle. Luka knew Lin’s bravado didn’t materially help his legal situation one bit, but at least now he didn’t feel alone anymore.

“Well, perhaps I shouldn’t keep you here. You better go rest. Today was tough,” she advised.

“I’m afraid I can’t, Sis. I still have a meetup with that officer I told you about…” He realized his mistake a second too late. He wasn’t sure if Nadya wanted the meeting kept private, but he had already spilled the beans to one of the most inquisitive journalists in Primorye.

“Ooh, a meetup or a date?” Lin teased, smiling from cheek to cheek.

“Just a meetup. We only met this morning. Besides, I don’t even know what she wants to talk about.” Since the cat was already out of the bag, he gave in and shared it all.

“Hmm. Well, since neither of us is going home just yet…” The mischievous intent was unmistakable on her face. “Mind if I tag along?”

“If I say no, you’ll probably just lurk around outside the windows anyway,” Luka conceded with a dry sigh. “So fine, you can come. But if she asks for a private word, you promise you’ll leave us alone, okay, Sis?”

“Of course! You won’t see a trace of me for the rest of the day, cross my heart.”

Luka nodded, and together they stood up and set off toward the bus stop.

Cafe Stela was a thirty-minute ride on the municipal transit line. It took its name from the polished steel monument standing beside the highway running into Pogranichny—_stela_ simply meaning monument in Russian. It couldn’t get much more straightforward than that. Still, because the spherical pylon looked so remarkably like a classic lollipop, almost all the locals referred to both the monument and the establishment as “Chupa-Chups.”

When they arrived, the cafe was already bustling with patrons. This was no obscure alleyway coffee shop; with its warm aerogel glass facade and modern interior, it drew in locals and travelers alike. The menu covered everything from tea and fresh pastries to hearty lunches and second-floor dining. Calling it a simple cafe felt like an understatement.

“I wonder if she remembered to book a table…” Seeing the crowded foyer, Luka started to worry about seating.

They stepped through the double doors into the humid, welcoming heat and were greeted by the hostess. “Welcome to Cafe Stela, dear guests. Do you have a reservation?”

“Is there a table reserved under the name Nadezhda Morozova?” Luka asked politely.

“Let me check…” The hostess tapped her screen. “…No, I don’t see a booking under that name, I’m afraid.”

Luka and Lin exchanged a knowing glance, having suspected as much.

“In that case, could we put in a reservation under that name now?” Luka requested.

“Certainly. The reservation is logged. It will take about an hour for a table to open up, but you’re welcome to wait in the snack bar in the meantime,” the hostess replied, gesturing toward an arched doorway.

“Thank you, miss,” Luka said, leading Lin toward an empty corner booth.

The good thing about an all-rounder restaurant like this was the communal snack and salad bar. Before the Time Ripple, you could technically walk in off the street, fill yourself up, and leave without even ordering anything. Still, most people had the common decency not to rely solely on the snack bar if they had the means to order from the cafe. Since the Time Ripple had disrupted the supply chain, the snack bar had switched to by-order only, deducting a nominal amount from a citizen's weekly food credits.

After a few snacks and casual conversation, a familiar figure hurried through the entrance. Nadya stepped inside, hurriedly scanning the room. Luka spotted her first and raised a hand to wave. Catching sight of them, she quickly made her way over to their booth.

“Sorry I’m late! And I completely forgot to make a reservation!” she apologized breathlessly.

“Don’t worry, it’s fine. I already booked one under your name,” Luka said, checking the time on his phone. “Should be ready soon. It’s been about forty-five minutes.”

Nadya unbuttoned her navy wool peacoat and draped it over the back of an empty seat. She had clearly stopped by her house to change off-duty; beneath the coat, she wore a clean black turtleneck and dark blue trousers, letting her hair fall naturally past her collar.

Once seated, her eyes drifted curiously across the table. “And… who might this lady be?”

Luka looked to Lin, letting her take the lead on introductions.

“Hello! I’m Lin Chen, a reporter with the _Heilongjiang People’s Daily_ and a close friend of Luka,” Lin said, extending a hand across the table. “Glad to make your acquaintance, Comrade Morozova.”

Nadya shook her hand warmly. “Likewise, though please just call me Nadya. And you?”

“Lin is fine, thank you,” she replied with an easy smile before releasing her grip.

Unsure of how much had been shared, Nadya glanced between them cautiously. “So… have you two already spoken about…?”

“Don’t worry, I told her everything,” Luka assured her. “That’s why she’s here.”

“Oh! Alright then.” Nadya closed her eyes and let out a long, deliberate breath.

When she opened them, her expression was filled with genuine remorse. “First of all, Luka, I am truly sorry. I never intended to drag you into a mess like this…” She took full responsibility for his impending legal trouble.

“Please, don’t blame yourself. None of us saw it coming,” Luka said, harboring no resentment toward her.

“I know how strict Major Tarasov is, but I didn’t think he would escalate things so fast.”

“To be honest, I don’t blame him either,” Luka admitted. “From his perspective, he has every right to be suspicious.”

Nadya shook her head. “Maybe on the surface. But on the drive back to the station, I thought about our interactions this morning, and his theory just stopped making sense.”

She leaned forward, resting her forearms on the table. “I was the one who brought up Igor’s burned car. I was the one who dragged you out to the scrapyard. There’s no way you could have predicted that. And once we got close to the scene, you never crossed the police tape or touched a single thing.”

She looked directly at Luka, her voice firm. “Then you turned around and volunteered critical information that obviously made you look guilty. If you really were an accomplice trying to stage a fake death with a burned body, that would be the dumbest plan in history. Nobody goes through all that trouble to trick the Militsiya just to hand us the real victim's name on a silver platter.”

Luka nodded quietly. It was a relief to know that despite everything, their brief time together had earned him her genuine trust.

Their conversation was interrupted by the host calling out their reservation number. They gathered their coats and moved over to their reserved table in the main cafe area.

Once their drinks and pastries were served, Lin broke the silence.

“As much as we trust Luka, the clock is already ticking. How long until the autopsy report arrives from Vladivostok?”

Nadya thought for a moment. “Hard to tell, but it shouldn't take more than two days…”

Two days. A mere forty-eight hours. Their trust was nothing against official forensic evidence. A heavy mood settled over the table once again.

“Can we find something in the meantime to prove Luka’s innocence?” Lin asked.

“The precinct won't find any physical link connecting Luka to the crime,” Nadya replied, “but we should try to stop this from escalating into an arrest in the first place.”

“Yeah, I think so too,” Lin agreed, turning toward Luka. “Do you have an alibi? Anything or anyone that can confirm where you were last night?”

“Nothing, I’m afraid,” Luka said with a self-deprecating shrug. “The CCTV around my dorm is still down from the Ripple, and I was asleep in bed. Alone, per usual.” The joke fell flat against the quiet table.

An awkward silence hung over them until Nadya’s eyes suddenly lit up.

“Wait. I have an idea…”

She reached into her leather crossbody bag and pulled out a fresh, unwritten roll of e-paper. She unrolled it flat against the tabletop, smoothing the surface with her palm before looking up at Luka.

“Alright, Luka. I don’t want to abuse your power, but what if I swear on my mother’s life that the moment we solve this case, I will write the final case summary right on this e-paper?” She laid out her idea with steady confidence. “If you look into the future of this sheet right now, whatever I write should already be there. It could give us a lead before time runs out.”

Luka looked down at the blank surface. If what she proposed was actually possible, it would make his ability the most powerful tool on Earth. Yet the idea of seeing the future so easily felt deeply unsettling. As a scientist, his whole life was built on testing hypotheses through observation and error. Skipping straight to the answer felt almost wrong.

Still, with forty-eight hours standing between him and a jail cell, they had run out of conventional options.

“_We’ll never know if we never try…_” Luka thought.

He leaned forward, focused his gaze on the clean, blank sheet of e-paper, and let his vision take over as the world around him began to blur…






